# Combobox / Autocomplete

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A combobox helps someone choose or enter a value from a long list. They type to narrow the list, then pick an item. Use it when a plain select would be too long to scan, such as a country, an icon or an area.

Choose in this order:

1. A native [select](select.html) when the list is short and fixed.
2. A [text input](text-input.html) with a suggestion list when people may type something that is not on the list.
3. The Home Assistant picker when people must pick one real item from a long list and cannot invent one.

This page covers:

- Combobox / Autocomplete
  - `<datalist>`
  - `ha-picker-combo-box`
  - `ha-generic-picker`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A visible label, and helper text that says what the choice changes.
- A field that shows the current choice, or a button that opens the list.
- A way to narrow the list by typing.
- The list of items. In Home Assistant each item has a name, an optional second line, and an icon or picture.
- A way to clear the choice or close the list without choosing.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-datalist"><code>&lt;datalist&gt;</code></h3>

The HTML element for suggestions. It holds `option` elements and is attached to an input by the input’s `list` attribute.

- It only suggests. Someone can still type any value, unlike a `select`.
- Each option shows its `value`, and an optional `label`.
- It works with text, search, URL, telephone, email and number inputs, and with some other input types.
- The browser draws the suggestion list. You cannot style it.
- Browsers and assistive technologies differ in how they present it, so test the ones your household uses.
- The input still needs its own visible label.

Home Assistant does not use `datalist` in the dashboard. It is the plain-HTML choice for a Civica documentation page. This site uses it for [Search autocomplete](../publishing/components/search-autocomplete.html).

<h3 id="child-ha-picker-combo-box"><code>ha-picker-combo-box</code></h3>

Home Assistant draws its searchable selection lists with the generic picker, described next. The picker holds this searchable list, whose rows are `ha-combo-box-item`, and a closed field called `ha-picker-field`. You cannot add any of these to a dashboard on their own. Use a picker, a selector in a form, or the native `select`.

<h3 id="child-ha-generic-picker"><code>ha-generic-picker</code></h3>

The picker behind most “choose one from a long list” fields in Home Assistant: floors, areas, labels, users, icons, countries, currencies and units of measurement. It is not a card type, so you cannot write it in dashboard YAML. People meet it in forms and dialogs.

What people see:

- A closed field, which is a button that shows the label and the current choice. It has a Clear control when a choice is set.
- On a wide screen the list opens in a popover. On a narrow or short screen it opens in a bottom sheet.
- A search field takes focus when the list opens. Typing narrows the list. Enter picks the top match.
- Some pickers show filter chips above the list to narrow it by section.
- Some pickers offer an “Add custom item” row, where a value that is not on the list is allowed.
- Escape closes the list.
- Each item can show a second line of detail, so two items with the same name can be told apart.

What a form author can set in Home Assistant’s own code includes the label, placeholder, helper text, whether a value is required, and a message when nothing matches. Dashboard authors choose these only through the form or selector they are using.

Variables in the Civica theme that reach it: `primary-color`, the `ha-color-fill-*` and `ha-color-border-*` keys behind the selected and active row colours, and `error-color` for the invalid helper text. The picker’s width, maximum width and popover height are Home Assistant’s own. Changing them needs the card-mod integration or custom code, and the Civica theme does not set them.

Home Assistant does not say that the search field and list follow the combobox pattern in the WAI-ARIA guidance, so do not describe it that way. Call it a searchable list in a dialog. Test it with the keyboard and your screen reader.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A long list becomes usable because people can type to narrow it.
- A `datalist` needs no script, but you cannot style it and the support differs between browsers.
- A `datalist` can hide the label if the author forgets it. Keep the label visible.
- The Home Assistant picker adapts to a narrow screen by opening a sheet, but you cannot restyle it with a theme alone.
- The picker is not available as a card, so there is no YAML for it.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

- Write the label as the task: “Which area is this for?” not “Area”.
- Say what the choice changes in helper text, before the list opens.
- Show the current choice in words in the closed field.
- Add a second line when two items could look the same, for example the floor under an area name.
- Say what happens when nothing matches, and whether a custom value is allowed.
- Never rely on an icon or colour alone to tell items apart.
- Do not move focus away while someone is typing.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions: the field has a visible label.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value and 2.1.1 Keyboard: the field and the list work from the keyboard.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible: focus stays visible in the field and the list.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: it works at 320 CSS pixels, where the sheet replaces the popover.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: a picker saves retyping a name that already exists.

